Summary: Keep an abandoned mutex alive while its waiters are woken, so wineserver doesn't crash
Category: sync
Kind: fix
Origin: wine-mac-builds
  Note: WineHQ fixed the same use-after-free in 10.12 by having an owned
    mutex hold a reference to itself (934514db, "server: Keep owned mutex
    syncs alive until abandoned"), after it had split the mutex's sync
    state into an object of its own. Ported to 10.0, where the mutex is
    still the named object, that reference would also keep an owned
    mutex's name reachable after its last handle is closed, so this fix
    holds a reference only while abandon_mutexes() wakes the waiters.
Upstream: differently (934514db, Wine 10.12)
Needed until: 10.12 (WineHQ 934514db)
Why: On 10.0 (WineHQ's release, without msync or esync), when a thread
 exits owning a mutex whose last handle was closed while another thread
 waited on it, the waiter's wait is the mutex's last reference. Abandoning
 the mutex wakes the waiter, the end of its wait frees the mutex, and
 wake_up() then reads the freed mutex's wait queue to go on: wineserver
 crashes ("wineserver crashed, please enable coredumps") and every
 program in the prefix exits with it. AddressSanitizer on 10.0's
 wineserver: heap-use-after-free in wake_up (thread.c), freed by
 end_wait under the same wake_up, from abandon_mutexes and kill_thread.
 abandon_mutexes() now holds a reference to each mutex across
 do_release(). 11.x doesn't crash (an owned mutex holds a reference
 there).
Programs: none known; a program where one thread closes a mutex's last
 handle while another waits on it and the owner then exits or is
 terminated (WineHQ's kernel32 sync test does this since 10.12)
Seen on: macOS 26.7.2 (M1 Max, 10.0: tests/regress/abandoned-mutex, 8 of 8
 runs crashed wineserver, i686 and x86_64)
Fine on: macOS 26.7.2 (M1 Max, 11.22: 3 of 3 runs passed without the patch)
Related: ntdll-msync; with WINEMSYNC or WINEESYNC its own abandon path
 handles these mutexes
Status: 10.0: 2026-11-26: written; not yet in a build. Its wineserver,
 built with AddressSanitizer outside the build, passed abandoned-mutex 7
 of 7 (i686 and x86_64), with no ASan report; without the fix 4 of 4 were
 reported as heap-use-after-free

diff --git a/server/mutex.c b/server/mutex.c
--- a/server/mutex.c
+++ b/server/mutex.c
@@ -142,7 +142,11 @@
         assert( mutex->owner == thread );
         mutex->count = 0;
         mutex->abandoned = 1;
+        /* a waiter's reference may be the last one, and waking it ends its
+         * wait; keep the mutex until wake_up() is done with its wait queue */
+        grab_object( mutex );
         do_release( mutex );
+        release_object( mutex );
     }
 }
 
